<span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;color:#0000FF;">sorry</span>
</div>
</div>
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">problem:
<br class="ltx_break"/></span>NT4. If the positive integers <math alttext="x" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S7.SS1.p4.m1"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math alttext="y" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S7.SS1.p4.m2"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are such that both <math alttext="3x+4y" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S7.SS1.p4.m3"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3x+4y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math alttext="4x+3y" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S7.SS1.p4.m4"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4x+3y</annotation></semantics></math> are perfect squares, prove that both <math alttext="x" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S7.SS1.p4.m5"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math alttext="y" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S7.SS1.p4.m6"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are multiples of 7.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent" id="S7.SS1.p5">
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3 (Algebra):
<br class="ltx_break"/>Lean 4 statement:</span></p>
